\begin{abstract}
We introduce an original mathematical framework that extends the classic Collatz dynamical system to rational fractions. By bridging physical 1D slicing geometry with abstract parity properties, we define fraction parity via component cut requirements ($C = X - 1$). We present a comprehensive taxonomy across five structural configurations (Control Baseline, Versions 1--4), demonstrating trajectories ranging from infinite denominator divergence traps to bounded decay loops and chaotic reciprocal crossover shifts. Furthermore, statistical analysis of Version 4 reveals a strictly locked, dual-phase deterministic oscillator driven by an invariant 2-step internal parity clock.
\end{abstract}

\section{Core Vision \& The Foundational Axiom}
In classical number theory, parity is strictly defined for whole integers. The Carrot-Collatz System extends parity evaluation to rational fractions $N/D$ by representing components as discrete spatial divisions on a 1D line segment.

\begin{axiom}[The "Cuts Plus One" Rule]
For any integer value $X$, the number of required physical parallel cuts $C$ to produce $X$ discrete segments is given by:
\begin{equation}
C = X - 1
\end{equation}
\end{axiom}

\begin{definition}[Parity Determination]
A component's parity state is governed by the parity of its cut requirement $C$:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Even $C \implies$ \textbf{EVEN} operational step.
    \item Odd $C \implies$ \textbf{ODD} operational step.
\end{itemize}
When evaluating joint parity across both numerator and denominator, total cuts are expressed as:
\begin{equation}
C_{\text{Total}} = (N - 1) + (D - 1) = N + D - 2
\end{equation}
\end{definition}

