\section{Detailed Technical Analysis}

\subsection{Part 0: The Baseline Control (Numerator-Only Parity)}
Evaluates parity strictly via $C_N = N - 1$. An odd numerator yields an even cut count, executing the division step. An even numerator yields an odd cut count, executing $\times 3 + 1$. The odd rule calculation instantly clears the denominator out entirely, transforming the fraction into a whole integer and matching the standard integer Collatz path down to 1.

\subsection{Part 1: Version 1 (Denominator-Only Parity)}
An odd starting denominator $D$ gives an even cut count $C_D = D - 1$, triggering fraction division by 2 ($D \to 2D$). Because doubling any odd number yields an even integer, subsequent cuts $C'_D = 2D - 1$ are permanently odd. The system locks on the Odd Rule, inflating $N \to \infty$ while leaving $D$ stagnant.

\subsection{Part 2: Version 2 (The Joint-Cut Expansion)}
Uses total cuts $C_{\text{Total}} = N + D - 2$. The sequence eventually degrades to $D = 2$ (contributing 1 odd cut). Algebraic progression forces the numerator to remain permanently odd. Since an even numerator cut count plus an odd denominator cut count is strictly odd, the system locks on the odd path to infinity.

\subsection{Part 3: Version 3 (The Denominator Squeeze)}
Re-engineers transformations to stabilize trajectory entropy. Even steps execute $D_{\text{new}} = 3D + 1$, while Odd steps execute $N_{\text{new}} = N / 2$, compressing values into bounded decay loops.

\subsection{Part 4: Version 4 (The Chaotic Cross-Over Shift)}
Enforces a dynamic reciprocal inversion on even steps:
\begin{equation}
\frac{N}{D} \longrightarrow \frac{N}{2D} \longrightarrow \frac{2D}{N}
\end{equation}
Paired with the modular odd rule $\frac{3N - D}{D}$, values continuously cross over fractional boundary limits, destroying fixed deterministic traps and inducing chaotic bouncing.

\section{Empirical Randomness \& Trajectory State Analysis}
To evaluate whether Version 4 behaves as a pseudo-random number generator (PRNG) or a deterministic chaotic map, we subjected its normalized output streams to statistical randomness tests adapted from the NIST SP 800-22 benchmark suite.

\subsection{Internal Parity Clock \& State Locking}
Initial evaluations using threshold extraction ($b_t = 1$ if $N_t > D_t$, else $0$) and modulo extraction ($b_t = (N_t + D_t) \pmod 2$) across $5{,}000$ iterations produced a Monobit test $p$-value of $1.0000$ with an exact $50/50$ zero-one distribution ($2{,}500$ zeros, $2{,}500$ ones). However, both tests yielded a Lag-1 autocorrelation coefficient of $r = -1.0000$ and a Runs test failure ($p = 0.0000$).

\begin{proposition}[Version 4 Strict Parity Periodicity]
Under Version 4 transformation rules, the reduced numerator parity $N_t \pmod 2$ and joint cut parity $(N_t + D_t - 2) \pmod 2$ form a strictly coupled 2-step period:
\begin{equation}
N_{t+1} \pmod 2 = 1 - (N_t \pmod 2)
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
On even steps, the transformation $\frac{N}{D} \rightarrow \frac{2D}{N}$ forces $N_{\text{new}} = 2D$, guaranteeing an even numerator ($N_{\text{new}} \equiv 0 \pmod 2$). On the subsequent odd step, the transformation $N_{\text{new}} = 3N' - D'$ operates on an even $N'$ and an odd $D'$, producing an odd integer ($3N' - D' \equiv 1 \pmod 2$). Thus, the discrete state machine is topologically constrained to alternate operational states on every iteration.
\end{proof}

